<h3>Mitaka - Takao</h3>
Station-stasion.
Mitaka - Takao.
Jalur opat trek ngan nepi ka. Kiwari keur diwangun antara Mitaka jeung Tachikawa ku cara naekeun trek jeung ngurangan sisimpangan; bagéan jalur ieu bisa ngurangan kacilakaan salila jam sibuk. Saterusna diusulkeun nambahan dua trek nepi ka Tachikawa; sanajan kitu, itu teu kaasup trek di luhur, bakal rengse antara 2008-2011.
<h3>Takao - Shiojiri</h3>
Takao - Shiojiri.
Most of the rapid service trains from Tokyo turn at Takao where the line exits the large urban aréa of Tokyo. The section between Takao and Ōtsuki still carries some commuter trains as well as long distance local trains and express trains. The Kaiji limited express turns at Kōfu, the capital of &lt;a href="Yamanashi%20Prefecture"&gt;Yamanashi Prefecture&lt;/a&gt;, while the Azusa and Super Azusa continue beyond Shiojiri to the &lt;a href="Shinonoi%20Line"&gt;Shinonoi Line&lt;/a&gt;.
<h4>Okaya – Shiojiri</h4>
Okaya – Shiojiri.
The Okaya-Shiojiri branch is an old route of the Chūō Main Line. It carries a small number of shuttle trains and trains from/to the &lt;a href="Iida%20Line"&gt;Iida Line&lt;/a&gt;, which branches off at Tatsuno.
<h3>Shiojiri - Nakatsugawa</h3>
Shiojiri - Nakatsugawa.
Shiojiri is the dividing point of the éast Line and the West Line; no train continues from one to the other. The Shinano limited express is the main player of the rural Shiojiri-Nakatsugawa section.
